+/*
+ * BareMetal-Firecracker port.
+ *
+ * Upstream is hand-written asm ending in two raw `syscall` instructions
+ * (munmap the caller's own stack, then exit) -- same reason as
+ * arch/x86_64/syscall_arch.h's __bmos_syscall() calls (see that file):
+ * no syscall trap on this port, everything routes through a plain C
+ * dispatcher instead. This only runs for a *detached* thread tearing
+ * down its own stack right before exiting (see __pthread_exit() in
+ * pthread_create.c), so unlike a joinable thread's exit -- where the
+ * joiner is the one who eventually munmaps -- there is genuinely
+ * nobody else left to free this memory afterward.
+ *
+ * `call __bmos_syscall` (unlike a real `syscall` instruction) pushes a
+ * return address onto the very stack being unmapped here, which would
+ * be unsafe on real Linux (a real munmap() immediately invalidates
+ * that memory). It's safe on this port specifically because
+ * posix_shim.c's munmap() never actually invalidates anything -- freed
+ * ranges just join an address-sorted free list for later mmap() calls
+ * to reuse (see its own comment) -- so the bytes stay live until
+ * something else actually allocates over them. `cli` closes the one
+ * real gap that leaves: thread_shim.c's timer-tick scheduler could
+ * otherwise preempt this thread *between* the munmap and the final
+ * exit and hand this exact memory to another thread's mmap() while
+ * still running on it. Left disabled deliberately -- SYS_exit's
+ * handler (thread_shim_exit_current()) takes over the same discipline
+ * from here (it disables interrupts itself immediately) and only
+ * re-enables them once execution has moved to a different thread's
+ * stack entirely.
+ */
